<Vertical id="NumberLineInputDisplayDemo" title="A position the learner does not set">
<Markdown id="display_intro">
### A running position, drawn on the same scale

`display="true"` turns the block around: it **shows** a position computed by
`initial=` instead of taking one. There is no control to drag — the marker is
a picture of a number — and the field is never written.

Answer either item below and the marker appears; answer both and it moves to
the average. Items nobody has answered are skipped by `average()`, so the
position means something from the first answer on.
</Markdown>

<ChoiceInput id="d1">
  <Key id="d1_strongly_agree" value="strongly_agree" code="2">Strongly agree</Key>
  <Key id="d1_agree" value="agree" code="1">Agree</Key>
  <Key id="d1_disagree" value="disagree" code="-1">Disagree</Key>
  <Key id="d1_strongly_disagree" value="strongly_disagree" code="-2">Strongly disagree</Key>
</ChoiceInput>
<Markdown id="d1_prompt">*I write to show what I already know.* — recorded as **{{@d1.code}}**</Markdown>

<ChoiceInput id="d2" reverseCoded="true">
  <Key id="d2_strongly_agree" value="strongly_agree" code="-2">Strongly agree</Key>
  <Key id="d2_agree" value="agree" code="-1">Agree</Key>
  <Key id="d2_disagree" value="disagree" code="1">Disagree</Key>
  <Key id="d2_strongly_disagree" value="strongly_disagree" code="2">Strongly disagree</Key>
</ChoiceInput>
<Markdown id="d2_prompt">*I write to work out what I think.* (reverse-coded) — recorded as **{{@d2.code}}**</Markdown>

<!-- The arithmetic, written once in the content that depends on it:

       code   average   50 + 25 * average
        -2      -2              0    both items at the first pole
        -1      -1             25
         0       0             50    the middle of the line
         1       1             75
         2       2            100    both items at the second pole

     Codes run -2..2 and the line runs 0..100, so the map is a scale of 25
     and a shift of 50. The reversed item already carries reversed codes
     (d2 is reverseCoded), so a PLAIN average is correct — nothing is
     negated here. `average()` skips unanswered items, and is absent when
     none are answered: that absence is what draws the placeholder instead
     of a marker. -->
<NumberLineInput id="display_why" min="0" max="100" step="1" snap="step" showValue="true"
                 title="Why I write" display="true" markerLabel="you"
                 initial="50 + 25 * average([@d1.code, @d2.code])"
                 placeholder="Your position appears as you answer.">
  <Tick value="0">Thinking</Tick>
  <Tick value="100">Showing</Tick>
</NumberLineInput>

<Markdown id="display_outro">
The same block without `display=` is the input the learner answers on, so a
course can show a computed position on exactly the scale its questions were
asked on — one visual vocabulary, not two.
</Markdown>
</Vertical>
